sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch04-05> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Simple rules acting only on whole numbers (such as multiplying by 5/2 when even and adding 1 then halving when odd) can produce sequences whose overall sizes fluctuate in a complex, apparently random way with no sign of repetition, so complexity is not just an artifact of looking at digits.";
    dc:type "computational-observation";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Simple whole-number rules can yield complex sizes, not just complex digits";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 4, “Systems Based on Numbers”; section “Elementary Arithmetic” (from p. 117), p. 124. Scope: simple piecewise arithmetic maps on whole numbers"
}
